Boolean algebra  is a formalism for manipulating truth values 1 and 0

Boolean operators are conjunction, disjunction and negation.

We present complex expressions by using these operators.

Ex : (¬(1 ∨ 0)) ∧ (1 ∨ (1 ∧ 0))

Propositional logic is a formalism on combining propositions and make conclusions about their truth value. 

An Atomic proposition is a single fact or property

Since propositional logic is a language we need to specify its syntax and semantics.

Syntax is which expressions are allowed
Semantics are what they mean

Syntax : take a set of propositional variables (x,y,z), these are atomic propositions

	The class/set of propositional formulas are defined as
1.	Every propositional variable is a formula
2.	If A and B are formulas then their conjunction, negation and disjunction are also formulas
Operator precedence: not, and, or

Semantics : Atomic propositions can be true or false, hence the truth status of propositional formulas also depend on its components
	A valuation is the assignment of a truth value to each variable
	Example v(z) = 1

The three kinds of propositional formulas are : 

1.	Tautologies : True regardless the valuation
2.	Contradictions : False regardless of valuation
3.	Non tautological satisfiable : Exists a truth status but not true for every valuation

Equivalence is where two formulas result in the same truth table. 

The completeness of operators are shown by the fact that the operators suffice to express any formula regardless the number of variables.

De Morgan
¬ (X ^ Y) = ¬ X v ¬ Y
¬ (X v Y) = ¬ X ^ ¬ Y
Distributivity
X ^ ( Y v Z) = (X ^ Y) v (X ^ Y)
X v (Y ^ Z) = (X v Y) ^ (X v Y)
Involution
¬ ¬ X = X


